CyberArk Management Approach
Database-Owned Active Directory Service Accounts
SQL Server | Oracle | MongoDB | PostgreSQL


Document Owner	PAM Engineering
Document Status	Draft - Stakeholder Review
Version	0.1
Date	August 10, 2026
Primary Reference	CyberArk Blueprint - Database Built-In Admins
Purpose: establish a repeatable approach for onboarding and lifecycle management of Active Directory service accounts owned by database teams.
 
1. Purpose and Scope
This document defines the proposed CyberArk approach for Active Directory service accounts that are owned by database teams and used by SQL Server, Oracle, MongoDB, or PostgreSQL services. The intent is to bring these identities under consistent PAM governance without treating them as database-local users when the password is actually controlled by Active Directory.
The scope covers discovery, account classification, Safe placement, platform selection, password rotation, dependent account management, verification, session controls, audit requirements, operational prerequisites, and stakeholder ownership. Database-local accounts such as SQL logins, Oracle local users, PostgreSQL roles with passwords, or MongoDB local users are referenced where relevant, but they are a separate credential-management use case.
1.1 User Story
As a PAM Engineer, I want to research and create a documented approach to onboard and manage database-owned Active Directory service accounts used by SQL Server, Oracle, MongoDB, and PostgreSQL within CyberArk, so that the organization has a standardized, secure, and repeatable process for privileged account onboarding and lifecycle management, reducing operational risk and supporting PAM governance requirements.
1.2 Business Justification
Database-owned Active Directory service accounts may run database services, agents, schedulers, backup processes, monitoring components, integration jobs, or other supporting workloads. When these accounts are excluded from PAM governance, passwords are more likely to remain static, ownership can become unclear, and password changes may be avoided because service dependencies are not fully understood. A controlled onboarding model allows the organization to centralize credential custody, establish accountable ownership, rotate passwords safely, verify credentials, and produce auditable evidence of account management.
1.3 Acceptance Criteria
ID	Acceptance Criterion	Covered In
AC1	Research across SQL Server, Oracle, MongoDB, and PostgreSQL covering discovery methods, CPM plugins/platforms, platform configuration, and connection/session options.	Sections 4-8
AC2	Documented strategy covering platform selection, naming conventions, Safe design, and CPM policy.	Sections 3, 6, 7
AC3	Prerequisites and dependencies identified.	Section 8
AC4	Lifecycle management covering rotation, dependency synchronization, session controls, audit, and governance.	Sections 7, 9
AC5	Stakeholder review and approval path defined.	Section 10
2. Recommended Approach
Design decision: database ownership does not change the credential authority. If the identity is an Active Directory account, CyberArk must manage the password in Active Directory.
For the accounts in scope, the recommended target account platform is a controlled clone of CyberArk's Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP platform, with one platform defined for each database service family. This allows separate password and operational policies while preserving the correct password-change mechanism against Active Directory. CyberArk currently recommends Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP for Windows domain account management, with Verify, Change, and Reconcile support. [2]
The Microsoft SQL Server, Oracle Database, MongoDB, and database ODBC CPM plugins are relevant when CyberArk is managing a password that exists inside the database engine. They should not be used to change an Active Directory service account password simply because the database team owns the account. SQL Server and Oracle have documented native database password-management plugins, and CyberArk also provides an ODBC framework for other database technologies. [8][9][11]
2.1 Proposed Platform Model
Database Service	Proposed AD Service Account Platform	Password Authority	Native DB Plugin Role
SQL Server	WIN-DOMAIN-DB-SQL-SVC	Active Directory via LDAP	Use only for SQL-authenticated database-local accounts.
Oracle	WIN-DOMAIN-DB-ORA-SVC	Active Directory via LDAP	Use only for Oracle-local database accounts.
MongoDB	WIN-DOMAIN-DB-MONGO-SVC	Active Directory via LDAP	Use native/Marketplace database plugin only for MongoDB-local accounts after validation.
PostgreSQL	WIN-DOMAIN-DB-POSTGRES-SVC	Active Directory via LDAP	Use a supported database plugin/ODBC approach only for PostgreSQL-local password accounts after validation.
The platform names above are proposed working names. The final names should follow the organization's existing platform naming standard and be approved during implementation design.
2.2 Safe Naming
Database Service	Safe Name	Status
SQL Server	IN-LOCAL-SQL	Defined
Oracle	IN-LOCAL-ORA	Defined
PostgreSQL	IN-LOCAL-PostGreSQL	Defined
MongoDB	TBD	Recommended: IN-LOCAL-MongoDB for consistency, subject to approval
Safe access should be granted to the PAM operational groups, the appropriate database owner group, and required automation identities using least-privilege Safe permissions. Routine application users should not receive credential retrieval rights simply because they support the same application.
3. CyberArk Best-Practice Alignment
The CyberArk Blueprint for database privileged accounts recommends starting with an accurate inventory of database instances, using discovery capabilities to identify privileged accounts, creating Safes aligned with the access-control design, vaulting credentials, rotating passwords, verifying them regularly, isolating privileged sessions, and recording/auditing privileged activity. [1]
That guidance is used here as the baseline, with one important adaptation: the Blueprint page is written primarily for database built-in administrators, while this document addresses non-human Active Directory service accounts. For a service identity, password rotation must be coordinated with every dependent service or stored use of the credential before automatic management is enabled.
Blueprint Principle	Application to Database-Owned AD Service Accounts
Inventory before onboarding	Start from CMDB/DBA inventory and AD account ownership records. Identify each server, database instance, environment, and consuming service.
Discovery and classification	Use AD/domain discovery and host dependency discovery as the primary mechanisms; use database discovery as enrichment where supported.
Vault and rotate	Vault the AD account in the database-specific Safe and enable rotation only after all dependencies are mapped and tested.
Verify regularly	Enable scheduled Verify through the AD platform. CyberArk Blueprint recommends weekly verification for database privileged credentials; adopt this unless enterprise policy requires a different interval. [1]
Isolate privileged access	Service accounts should not be used for normal interactive administration. Human database administration should use separate named privileged identities through PSM/SIA.
Audit and monitor	Audit all retrievals, CPM actions, reconciliations, Safe membership changes, manual password operations, and privileged administrative sessions.
3.1 Password Policy Position
CyberArk's database built-in administrator Blueprint recommends strong 30-character passwords, a 30-day rotation interval (7 days for highly sensitive databases), and weekly verification. [1] For database-owned AD service accounts, these values should be treated as a security baseline rather than applied blindly. The final policy must also satisfy Active Directory password policy and the compatibility limits of every consuming service.
One-time password behavior is appropriate for human privileged access in some scenarios, but should not be enabled for a non-human runtime service account if it would force a password change after use or break dependent services. The service-account platform should use scheduled rotation with dependency synchronization instead.
4. Discovery and Account Identification
Discovery should be performed in layers. No single discovery source is expected to identify ownership, privilege, and all credential dependencies with enough confidence to enable automatic password rotation.
4.1 Primary Discovery Sources
Source	Purpose	Recommended Use
CMDB / database inventory	Establish the population of SQL Server, Oracle, MongoDB, and PostgreSQL services and their owners.	Primary scope source. CyberArk recommends beginning with IT inventory/CMDB for database discovery. [1]
Active Directory inventory	Identify service accounts, OU, group membership, password age, password-never-expires settings, owners, and disabled/locked state.	Primary identity source for AD-owned accounts.
CyberArk Windows/*nix discovery	Identify domain accounts present on servers and discover Windows service dependencies such as Windows Services, Scheduled Tasks, IIS pools, IIS directory security, and COM+.	Use against database server estates and supporting Windows servers. [5]
CyberArk database discovery	Identify privileged database users and privilege metadata.	Use as enrichment for SQL Server, Oracle, and PostgreSQL where applicable. Current database discovery supports SQL Server, Oracle, PostgreSQL, and MySQL; MongoDB is not listed as a database discovery scan type. [6]
DBA / application owner validation	Confirm business owner, actual runtime use, maintenance windows, cluster topology, failover nodes, and all consumers of the credential.	Mandatory before CPM rotation is enabled.
4.2 Discovery Workflow
1.	Obtain the authoritative list of database servers, clusters, instances, and service owners from CMDB and DBA inventories.
2.	Extract candidate Active Directory service accounts owned by the database teams. Include account name, distinguished name, owner, description, group membership, password settings, and last password-set date.
3.	Run CyberArk Windows discovery against the relevant Windows database servers and supporting hosts to identify where the AD accounts are used and to collect supported dependencies.
4.	Run database discovery scans for SQL Server, Oracle, and PostgreSQL where the Discovery service is available. Use the results to identify privileged database mappings and to distinguish Windows/external authentication from database-local authentication.
5.	For MongoDB, supplement CyberArk discovery with DBA/CMDB inventory and MongoDB configuration review because MongoDB is not currently listed among CyberArk's database discovery scan types. [6]
6.	Reconcile findings into an onboarding worksheet. Any account with an unknown owner, unknown dependency, unsupported usage, or unresolved cluster/failover behavior remains in 'Discovery/Assessment' status and is not enabled for automatic rotation.
4.3 Discovery Data to Capture
Field	Reason
Account Name / UPN / SAM Account Name	Unique identity and CyberArk onboarding key.
AD Domain / OU / Distinguished Name	Confirms directory authority and LDAP management scope.
Database Technology	Determines Safe, platform, owner group, and validation path.
Database Server / Cluster / Node	Identifies every host where the credential may be consumed.
Windows Service / Scheduled Task / Other Usage	Required to synchronize password changes safely.
Database Instance / Service Name	Supports ownership, impact analysis, and troubleshooting.
Owner / Support Group	Required for approval and incident response.
Environment / Criticality	Determines change controls and rotation schedule.
Password Never Expires / Password Age	Highlights unmanaged standing credentials and onboarding priority.
Interactive Logon Allowed	Should normally be disabled/restricted for service identities.
5. Account Classification
The onboarding decision is based on where the credential is authoritative, not on which team owns the account.
Account Type	Example	Correct CyberArk Management
AD service account used by a database service	DOMAIN\svc_sqlengine	Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP (or approved clone), plus dependent service usages.
SQL-authenticated SQL Server login	sql_backup_admin	Microsoft SQL Server database platform.
Oracle-local database account	APP_SCHEMA_ADMIN	Oracle Database platform.
MongoDB-local user	backupUser	Supported MongoDB/Marketplace database plugin after version and use-case validation.
PostgreSQL-local role with password	pg_backup	Supported PostgreSQL/ODBC database platform after plugin validation.
Human DBA account	named administrative identity	Separate privileged platform; access through PSM/SIA. Do not use the runtime service account for routine administration.
This distinction is important because CyberArk's SQL Server and Oracle database plugins connect to the database engine and execute database password-management operations, whereas the Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP plugin changes the password in Active Directory. [2][8][9]
6. Database-Specific Research and Proposed Configuration
6.1 SQL Server
•	Discovery: use AD/domain and Windows host discovery to locate the service identity and Windows Service dependencies. Use CyberArk SQL Server database discovery as a secondary source to identify privileged SQL logins and Windows/external mappings.
•	AD service account CPM platform: clone Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP into the approved SQL service-account platform. This is the platform that changes the AD password.
•	Native SQL CPM plugin: CyberArk documents a Microsoft SQL Server plugin with automatic password management for SQL Server accounts and ODBC connectivity. Use it only for database-local SQL credentials. [8]
•	Dependencies: SQL Server Database Engine, SQL Server Agent, SSRS/SSIS-related Windows services, cluster service usages, scheduled jobs, or other services that explicitly store the AD password must be identified before rotation.
•	Session access: if a human must connect to SQL Server using Windows Authentication, CyberArk provides an SSMS Windows Authentication PSM connection component. Routine human administration should use named admin accounts rather than the service identity. [13]
SQL Server is the strongest candidate for automated dependency onboarding because database services commonly run as Windows Services and CyberArk can synchronize Windows domain account passwords with dependent Windows Services. [3][4]
6.2 Oracle
•	Discovery: identify the AD service identity through AD and host/service inventory. Run Oracle database discovery to identify Oracle-local privileged users and external authentication metadata where useful.
•	AD service account CPM platform: use the Oracle-specific clone of Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP for any actual AD password credential.
•	Native Oracle CPM plugin: CyberArk provides an Oracle Database target account plugin for Oracle-local database users, with required Oracle client/ODBC components on the CPM. [9]
•	Dependencies: on Windows-hosted Oracle, identify Oracle services and any scheduled tasks or supporting processes running under the AD account. On Linux/Unix Oracle deployments, validate the actual authentication mechanism carefully; a Kerberos keytab, wallet, certificate, or OS account dependency is not equivalent to a Windows service password dependency.
•	Session access: PSM supports Oracle tools such as SQL*Plus, Toad, and Oracle SQL Developer for database sessions. CyberArk documentation notes that the Oracle SQL Developer connection client supports local database users and does not support domain users, so it should not be assumed to work for an AD service identity. [12][14]
6.3 MongoDB
•	Discovery: use CMDB/DBA inventory, AD inventory, and host/service discovery. CyberArk's current database discovery scan list includes SQL Server, Oracle, PostgreSQL, and MySQL, but not MongoDB. [6]
•	AD service account CPM platform: use the MongoDB-specific clone of Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP when the runtime identity is an AD user.
•	Native MongoDB password management: CyberArk release documentation identifies a MongoDB privilege-account management plugin and notes MongoDB 7 compatibility. The exact Marketplace package, supported versions, and change/reconcile behavior should be validated in the target tenant before it is used for MongoDB-local accounts. [10]
•	Dependencies: validate whether the MongoDB deployment runs as a Windows service under the AD account or uses a different mechanism on Linux. Only password-based usages that can be safely synchronized should be included in the automatic rotation design.
•	Session access: CyberArk SIA supports MongoDB database targets and includes both zero-standing-privilege and vaulted-access patterns. This is preferred for human database administration where SIA is part of the target architecture. [15]
6.4 PostgreSQL
•	Discovery: CyberArk supports PostgreSQL database discovery and can add discovered PostgreSQL users to the Discovered Accounts inventory. Use this in addition to AD and host discovery. [6]
•	AD service account CPM platform: use the PostgreSQL-specific clone of Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP for AD-owned service identities.
•	Native PostgreSQL password management: the current Privilege Cloud database plugin documentation does not expose a dedicated PostgreSQL target-account page in the same way as SQL Server and Oracle. CyberArk provides an ODBC database plugin framework for databases that support ODBC. Any PostgreSQL-local CPM solution should be validated against the current Marketplace package and supported driver/version before production use. [11]
•	Dependencies: PostgreSQL is frequently hosted on Linux, so confirm whether the AD identity is truly a password-based service identity, a Kerberos principal/keytab, an OS user, or simply an AD group used for database authorization. These require different PAM controls.
•	Session access: CyberArk SIA supports PostgreSQL database targets. Use named operator identities and controlled database access rather than interactive use of the runtime service account. [15]
7. Platform and CPM Policy
Each database service family will have its own approved platform clone so policy changes can be tested and rolled out independently without changing the password authority. The clones should inherit the Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP plugin and then be tuned for service-account lifecycle requirements.
Policy Area	Proposed Standard
Password authority	Active Directory through Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP.
Platform segregation	One platform for each database service family: SQL, Oracle, MongoDB, PostgreSQL.
Automatic management at onboarding	Disabled until dependency discovery, owner approval, and first rotation test are complete.
Password generation	Strong randomly generated password; target 30 characters where application and AD policy support it. Validate special-character compatibility before production.
Rotation interval	30-day baseline after successful pilot. Shorter intervals may be used for high-risk accounts only after dependency reliability is proven.
Verification	Weekly baseline, consistent with CyberArk database privileged account guidance. [1]
Reconciliation	Enabled using a dedicated least-privilege reconcile account with delegated reset/unlock rights where required. Avoid broad Domain Admin use.
Reset mode	Use only when operationally justified and tested against AD password-age rules and service dependencies.
Manual Change/Reconcile	Restricted to PAM operations. Manual actions require a change/incident reference for production accounts.
Interactive use	Not permitted for normal administration. Service identities are non-human runtime accounts.
Dependent accounts	All supported dependencies must be linked and healthy before automatic rotation is enabled.
7.1 Dependency Management
CyberArk dependent accounts represent services or resources that use the same credential as the target account. During a password change, CPM synchronizes the new password to the related dependent accounts. [3] For Windows-hosted database workloads, the Windows Services plugin is the primary dependency mechanism when a database service runs under an AD service identity. CyberArk recommends auto-detection for service dependencies where supported. [4]
Dependency Type	Use in this Standard
Windows Services	Primary mechanism for SQL Server and any Oracle/MongoDB/PostgreSQL service hosted on Windows under the AD identity.
Windows Scheduled Tasks	Use when jobs run under the service account and store its password.
IIS Application Pools / COM+ / IIS Directory Security	Include only if the database-owned account is actually used by these application components.
Database connection strings / database tables	Treat separately. CyberArk has a Database String dependent account platform for credentials stored in supported database tables, including MongoDB 7 in current documentation. [16]
Linux systemd / keytabs / wallets / certificates	Do not assume Windows dependency support. Confirm the actual secret type and use a supported/custom integration or redesign the authentication method.
7.2 Onboarding Sequence
1.  Confirm account ownership and business purpose.
2.  Confirm the account is an Active Directory account and identify the correct domain/OU.
3.  Assign the account to the appropriate database Safe.
4.  Assign the database-specific AD service account platform.
5.  Vault the current credential without triggering an immediate password change.
6.  Run Verify and confirm CyberArk can authenticate/manage the account through LDAP.
7.  Discover and link every supported dependency.
8.  Perform a dependency health check and capture a rollback plan.
9.  Schedule the first controlled password rotation during an approved maintenance window.
10.  Validate AD password update, dependent service update, service health, cluster/failover behavior, and application connectivity.
11.  Enable recurring automatic management only after successful validation and owner sign-off.
8. Prerequisites and Dependencies
Area	Requirement
Active Directory	The account must be a supported Windows domain user. Confirm OU location, password policy, service-logon rights, lockout behavior, and any gMSA eligibility.
LDAP security	Enable LDAP over SSL or StartTLS as required by the Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP plugin; install and trust required certificates on the CPM. [2]
Reconcile rights	Use a dedicated reconcile identity with delegated reset password rights and unlock permissions only where required by policy. [2]
CPM connectivity	CPM must reach the directory service and all dependent Windows hosts using the protocols required by the selected plugins.
Windows dependencies	Service Control Manager / remote service-management prerequisites must be met for Windows Service dependencies. Test Kerberos where used. [4]
Database discovery	Where used, configure CyberArk Connector Management, network reachability, the appropriate database drivers, scan credentials, and database permissions required by the scan type. [6]
Change window	First rotation must have a maintenance window, DBA/application owner participation, and documented rollback.
Clustering / HA	Identify active/passive and active/active nodes, virtual names, cluster resources, and any node-local credential stores.
Monitoring	Define service health checks that can prove the database and dependent components remain healthy after password rotation.
Backup / break-glass	Document how the credential can be recovered and how services can be restored if dependency synchronization fails.
8.1 Special Consideration: gMSA
Before onboarding a conventional password-based AD service account, the database team and AD engineering team should determine whether the workload can use a Group Managed Service Account (gMSA). A gMSA reduces the need to vault and rotate a manually managed password because Windows manages the credential automatically. Where the database product and operating model support gMSA, that option should be assessed as part of the service-account modernization path rather than creating a new long-lived password dependency.
9. Lifecycle Management and Governance
Lifecycle Stage	Control
Request	Account must have an owner, business purpose, database technology, environment, and approved Safe/platform mapping.
Onboard	Vault without immediate rotation, verify the credential, identify dependencies, and record the onboarding change.
Operate	CPM performs scheduled Verify and password rotation. Dependency failures are treated as operational events and investigated before further rotations.
Use	Runtime use is service-to-service. Human use of the service account is prohibited except approved break-glass procedures.
Monitor	Review CPM failures, repeated verification failures, password-age exceptions, retrieval events, Safe membership changes, and account lockouts.
Review	Periodic owner and entitlement recertification. Confirm the account is still required and that all dependencies remain accurate.
Offboard	Disable automatic management as part of controlled retirement, stop/remove dependent services, remove database privileges, disable/delete the AD account per IAM process, then remove/archive the CyberArk object.
9.1 Session Monitoring
The database service account itself should not be treated as a shared DBA login. Its normal purpose is non-interactive runtime authentication. Where DBAs require privileged database access, use separate named privileged identities and route those sessions through PSM or SIA where supported.
Database	Session Control Position
SQL Server	PSM supports SSMS using Database Authentication and Windows Authentication. For AD-based human access, use the Windows Authentication connection component. [13]
Oracle	PSM supports SQL*Plus, Toad, and Oracle SQL Developer for supported database-user scenarios. Oracle SQL Developer documentation notes a domain-user limitation. [12][14]
MongoDB	SIA supports MongoDB database access policies and vaulted/ZSP connection models. [15]
PostgreSQL	SIA supports PostgreSQL database access policies. [15]
9.2 Compliance Evidence
•	CyberArk account inventory showing Safe, platform, account owner, and automatic-management status.
•	CPM Verify/Change/Reconcile history.
•	Dependent account status and password synchronization results.
•	Safe membership and entitlement review records.
•	Password-age and password-never-expires exception reports from Active Directory.
•	Change records for initial onboarding, first rotation, platform changes, and service-impacting remediation.
•	PSM/SIA session records for human privileged access, where applicable.
•	Quarterly or periodic owner attestation that the service account and listed dependencies are still required.
10. Implementation and Stakeholder Approval
10.1 Pilot Approach
A small pilot should be completed for each database service family before bulk onboarding. Select accounts with known owners, a limited number of dependencies, non-critical initial workloads, and a clear rollback method.
Phase	Activity	Exit Criteria
1. Research validation	Confirm platform package/version, dependencies, connectivity, and authentication model.	PAM and DBA agree the design is technically valid.
2. Non-production pilot	Onboard one or more low-risk accounts and complete a controlled password rotation.	All dependent services remain healthy after rotation.
3. Failure test	Simulate or validate dependency failure handling and reconciliation/rollback.	Operations can restore service without manual password guessing or credential exposure.
4. Production pilot	Onboard a small production set during an approved change window.	Successful rotation and owner sign-off.
5. Scale	Move to repeatable onboarding waves and reporting.	KPIs and exception process are operational.
10.2 Stakeholders
Stakeholder	Responsibility
PAM Engineering	Platform build, Safe design, CPM configuration, onboarding, dependency management, evidence, operational support.
Active Directory / IAM	Directory policy, delegated reconcile permissions, service-account standards, gMSA assessment.
SQL Server DBA	SQL account inventory, service dependencies, cluster validation, change-window testing.
Oracle DBA	Oracle inventory, authentication design, service dependencies, database connectivity validation.
MongoDB DBA / Platform Team	MongoDB topology, authentication mechanism, plugin/SIA validation, service dependencies.
PostgreSQL DBA / Platform Team	PostgreSQL topology, AD/Kerberos use, plugin/SIA validation, service dependencies.
Application Owners	Business impact validation and application connectivity testing after rotation.
Information Security / GRC	Policy alignment, exception approval, compliance evidence requirements.
