11. Key Risks and Controls
Risk	Control
Password rotated before all dependencies are known	Keep automatic management disabled until dependency discovery, first-rotation testing, and owner approval are complete.
Wrong CPM platform selected because database team owns the account	Classify by password authority. AD accounts use the Windows Domain Accounts via LDAP plugin; database-local accounts use database plugins.
Service outage after password change	Use supported dependency plugins, maintenance windows, health checks, cluster testing, and a documented rollback plan.
Shared service account used interactively	Restrict interactive logon and use named privileged identities through PSM/SIA.
Broad reconcile privilege	Delegate only reset/unlock rights required for the managed OU; do not default to Domain Admin.
Unsupported Linux/Kerberos/keytab dependency	Do not force-fit a Windows password usage. Validate the authentication method and use the appropriate secret-management pattern.
MongoDB discovery gap	Use CMDB/DBA inventory and host discovery; validate MongoDB account inventory separately until native database discovery covers the required use case.
12. Definition of Done for Each Account
•	Account owner and support group are documented.
•	Database technology and all consuming servers are recorded.
•	Safe and platform assignment are correct.
•	Current credential is vaulted and Verify succeeds.
•	All supported dependent services are linked and healthy.
•	Unsupported or manual dependencies are documented with an approved operating procedure.
•	First password rotation has completed successfully in an approved window.
•	Application/database health checks pass after rotation and, where applicable, after cluster failover.
•	Automatic management is enabled only after owner sign-off.
•	Interactive use is restricted and the appropriate named-user PSM/SIA access path is defined.
•	The account is included in periodic PAM governance and owner recertification.
